    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p12-q13-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove Sophie Germain’s Identity,\n$$a^4+4b^4=(a^2+2b^2-2ab)(a^2+2b^2+2ab).$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The product equals $a^4+4b^4$.\n\nUse the difference of two squares, then expand and cancel the $4a^2b^2$ terms.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(a^2+2b^2-2ab)(a^2+2b^2+2ab)&=(a^2+2b^2)^2-(2ab)^2\\\\(a^2+2b^2-2ab)(a^2+2b^2+2ab)&=a^4+4a^2b^2+4b^4-4a^2b^2\\\\(a^2+2b^2-2ab)(a^2+2b^2+2ab)&=a^4+4b^4.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 45
      },
      {
        "id": "p12-q13-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Use this identity to express $3^4+(4\\times5^4)$ as a product of prime numbers.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$29\\times89$.\n\nSubstitute $a=3$ and $b=5$ into Sophie Germain’s Identity.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}3^4+4\\times5^4&=(3^2+2\\times5^2-2\\times3\\times5)(3^2+2\\times5^2+2\\times3\\times5)\\\\3^4+4\\times5^4&=(9+50-30)(9+50+30)\\\\3^4+4\\times5^4&=29\\times89.\\end{align*}$$\nNeither $29$ nor $89$ is divisible by a prime at most its square root: check $2,3,5$ for $29$, and $2,3,5,7$ for $89$. Both factors are prime.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 50
      }
    ]
  },
